{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the transplant team doctors. You were brought in from rehab because your potassium was very high. How are you feeling tonight?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel okay, actually. No chest pain or shortness of breath. Just tired. They said my potassium was high again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it was 7.2 when you arrived at the ER, which is quite high. But they treated it quickly with insulin, calcium, and Kayexalate. Your last check was down to 5.0, which is much safer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, I didn’t even know it could get that high without feeling sick. What caused it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The nephrology team thinks it’s due to dehydration. Your kidney function was a bit low when you came in, and not drinking enough fluids can cause potassium to build up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I guess I haven’t been drinking much. My mouth is dry, and I don’t have my dentures, so eating and drinking are hard."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We’re adjusting your tube feeding to a more diluted formula and increasing the rate to 60 cc per hour. That should help keep you hydrated and prevent this from happening again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that sounds good. I’ve been on tube feeds since the transplant, but it’s still hard to get enough in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re also continuing your immunosuppressants. Your tacrolimus level was very low, so we’ve switched you to sirolimus, which we discussed earlier. It might help with the tremors too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, the shaking in my hands has been worse. I thought it was just from being weak."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could be a mix of weakness and the medication. Sirolimus doesn’t usually cause tremors, so we’re hopeful this change will help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That would be a relief. I can barely hold a cup now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2194-09-06 23:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor it closely. For now, just rest. We’ll check your labs again in the morning to make sure your potassium stays stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I just reviewed your labs from 6:05 AM. Your potassium is now 5.0, and it dropped further to 3.8 by this morning’s check. That’s excellent progress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good news! Is it safe now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, 3.8 is within the normal range. Your kidney function also improved—creatinine is down to 0.8, which means you’re better hydrated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So the tube feeding change is helping?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Definitely. The increased fluid delivery is making a difference. We’ll keep it at this rate for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my diabetes? My sugar was high last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your glucose was 183 this morning, which is a bit high but not alarming. We’re monitoring it. Since you’re on tube feeds, we can adjust the formula if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I used to check it at home, but here it’s all managed for me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we check it regularly. No insulin was needed today, but we’ll keep an eye on it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my liver tests? Are they okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your liver enzymes are slightly elevated but stable. AST and ALT are coming down—from 86 and 70 to 70 and 45. That’s a good trend."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad. After the transplant, I worry about it every day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2194-09-07 07:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Totally understandable. But your liver is functioning well. The sirolimus should also help protect it long-term."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2194-09-07 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I just spoke with rehab. They have a bed available for you, and since you’re stable, we’re planning to transfer you back tomorrow morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2194-09-07 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So soon? I thought I might stay a few days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2194-09-07 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve responded really well. No symptoms, potassium is normal, and you’re hydrated. There’s no need to stay longer if you’re stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2194-09-07 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my tremors and eating? Will they help me with that at rehab?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2194-09-07 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, physical therapy will continue working on your strength and coordination. And the diet team will support your nutrition, especially without dentures."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2194-09-07 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I just wish I could eat more on my own."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2194-09-07 10:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep encouraging small oral intake when safe, but the tube feedings are ensuring you get enough calories and protein."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2194-09-07 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your afternoon labs are back—potassium is holding at 4.0, which is perfect. We’re on track for discharge to rehab tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2194-09-07 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. I was worried I’d be here longer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2194-09-07 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well. Just keep up with fluids and let the team know if you feel dizzy or unwell."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. We’re finalizing your transfer. Your vitals are stable, labs are good, and you’re cleared to go back to rehab."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I appreciate all the help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Keep up with the tube feedings, take your medications—including the new sirolimus—and follow up with the transplant clinic."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll ask rehab about getting help with dentures."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a great idea. Better dentures could really help with eating and comfort."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if my hands keep shaking, should I call you?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, or let rehab notify us. It may take a few days for the new medication to settle in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll do that. Thanks again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2194-09-08 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. Stay hydrated, keep up the rehab, and we’ll see you at your next appointment."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2194-09-06 23:10:00",
    "admission_end": "2194-09-08 18:00:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 72-year-old woman, status post orthotopic liver transplant for end-stage liver disease due to hepatitis C, was readmitted from rehabilitation with asymptomatic hyperkalemia (potassium 7.2) secondary to dehydration. She was treated in the emergency department with insulin, calcium gluconate, and Kayexalate, with potassium normalizing to 3.8 within 24 hours. Tube feedings were adjusted to a more dilute formula and increased rate to improve hydration. Her immunosuppressant was transitioned from tacrolimus to sirolimus due to low levels and persistent tremors. Liver function tests showed improvement, and diabetes was stable without insulin requirement during admission. She remained asymptomatic throughout, with stable vital signs and no complications. She was transferred back to rehabilitation on day two with a plan to continue monitoring tremors, nutrition, and medication adherence.",
    "problems": [
      "Hyperpotassemia",
      "Liver replaced by transplant",
      "Dehydration",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Disorder of bone and cartilage, unspecified",
      "Essential and other specified forms of tremor",
      "Nontoxic uninodular goiter",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of liver",
      "Personal history of peptic ulcer disease",
      "Personal history of other infectious and parasitic diseases"
    ]
  }
}
